[#4471](/kb/incidents/4471) says its reports are one bug with one fix queued. Read with the
tenant directory and the desk's own notes, they are three problems. One fix for the parser leaves
the other two open.

<Callout kind="warn">
#4471 is not one issue. Each problem below has its own ticket; count and fix them apart.
</Callout>

## The problems

<ProblemTable ticket={4471} />

- **`parser`**: the shared parser reads every date month first. A day-first tenant's dates
  come out with day and month swapped: 7 April is sent as 4 July. Every tenant, every channel.
- **`hint`**: booking-v3's form prints `MM/DD/YYYY` under its date field. People type what the
  hint says, and the form reads it the tenant's way, day first. Only tenants on booking-v3, only
  reports that came in through the form. Fixing the parser does not fix this one: the hint is wrong.
- **`shift`**: the date moves by one day, not by a month swap. "Tuesday 12 May" shows as
  "Monday 11 May"; one meeting went out as Tuesday in Lisbon and Monday in Toronto. That is a
  time zone bug. The desk noted these reports as a one-day move and counts them under `parser`
  by tenant; they stay counted there, and they get a ticket of their own.

## Why #4471 shows one

<CauseTable ticket={4471} />

- The desk merges by subject line ([decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject)).
  The same handful of subject lines turns up under all three problems, so subject alone cannot
  tell them apart. The rule's own consequences say different subjects split one bug; here, the
  same subjects joined three bugs.
- The table above files the one-day moves under `parser` and marks `hint` as "no ticket of its
  own". That is what the ticket shows, not what the reports say.
- Intake's tag is a guess from the reporter's browser. Count by tenant and channel, not by tag.
- The export repeats some rows. Count reports by id.

## For the desk

- Keep #4471 for `parser`. Move the other two to their own tickets.
- A report about a booking-v3 form is a `hint` report until its tenant says otherwise.
- A date that moved by one day is a `shift` report, whatever its subject.

